proof.text:
Use ordinary composition $v\circ u$ (first $u$, then $v$), and put $a=\alpha^{-1}$ and $\delta=\beta^{-1}$ as constructed in Step 1. For $c\in\mathcal C_1$, naturality of $\eta_2$ at $\eta_{1,c}$, naturality of $a$ at $\eta_{1,c}$, and the first triangle identity give, in that order,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\beta_{F_1c}\circ\eta_{1,c}
&=G_2(\varepsilon_{1,F_1c}\circ a_{G_1F_1c}\circ F_2(\eta_{1,c}))\circ\eta_{2,c}\\
&=G_2(\varepsilon_{1,F_1c}\circ F_1(\eta_{1,c})\circ a_c)\circ\eta_{2,c}
=G_2(a_c)\circ\eta_{2,c}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Postcomposing with $G_2(\alpha_c)$ gives $G_2(\alpha_c)\circ\beta_{F_1c}\circ\eta_{1,c}=\eta_{2,c}$. For the reversed unit identity, expand the formula for $\delta$ from Step 1. Naturality of $\eta_1$ at $\eta_{2,c}$, naturality of $\alpha$ at $\eta_{2,c}$, and the first triangle identity for $(F_2,G_2)$ give
$$
\begin{aligned}
\delta_{F_2c}\circ\eta_{2,c}
&=G_1(\varepsilon_{2,F_2c}\circ\alpha_{G_2F_2c}\circ F_1(\eta_{2,c}))\circ\eta_{1,c}\\
&=G_1(\varepsilon_{2,F_2c}\circ F_2(\eta_{2,c})\circ\alpha_c)\circ\eta_{1,c}
=G_1(\alpha_c)\circ\eta_{1,c}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Postcomposing with $G_1(a_c)$ gives $G_1(a_c)\circ\delta_{F_2c}\circ\eta_{2,c}=\eta_{1,c}$. These are precisely the forward and reversed unit compatibilities.
